        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(acpx): keep Codex wrapper error tail truncation UTF-16 safe",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n`readCodexWrapperStderrTail()` uses `text.slice(-6000)` for tail truncation. When a surrogate pair spans the 6000-char tail boundary, the result starts with an orphan low surrogate (`\\ude80`) producing `�`.\n\n**Proof on main:**\n```js\nconst input = \"🚀\" + \"x\".repeat(5999);   // length 6001\ninput.slice(-6000)[0] === \"\\ude80\"          // → true, orphan low surrogate\n```\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n1. Extracted `tailCodexWrapperError()` helper using `sliceUtf16Safe(text, -6000)`\n2. Added 3 test cases including surrogate-pair boundary proof\n\n## User Impact\n\n- Codex wrapper error diagnostics in ACPX keep emoji intact\n- No behavior change for ASCII text\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `tailCodexWrapperError` exported and tested directly\n- Boundary test constructs input where `slice(-6000)` on main produces a broken low surrogate; fix verifies first code point is never a low surrogate\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
